tion in obsessive-compulsive disorder</article-title><source>J Anxiety Disord</source><volume>15</volume><fpage>501</fpage><lpage>10</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">11764309</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b52-ndt-3-687"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yang</surname><given-names>YK</given-names></name><name><surname>Yu</surname><given-names>L</given-names></name><name><surname>Yeh</surname><given-names>TL</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Associated alterations of striatal dopamine D2/D3 receptor and transporter binding in drug-naive patients with schizophrenia: a dual-isotope SPECT study</article-title><source>Am J Psychiatry</source><volume>161</volume><fpage>1496</fpage><lpage>8</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15285982</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="b53-ndt-3-687"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Yoshizumi</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name><name><surname>Hurawe</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><name><surname>Honjo</surname><given-names>S</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2004</year><article-title>Hallucinatory experiences in a community sample of Japanese children</article-title><source>J Child Adolesc Psychiatry</source><volume>43</volume><fpage>1030</fpage><lpage>6</lpage></citation></ref><ref id="b54-ndt-3-687"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Zohar</surname><given-names>J</given-names></name><name><surname>Chopra</surname><given-names>M</given-names></name><name><surname>Sasson</surname><given-names>Y</given-names></name><etal/></person-group><year>2000</year><article-title>Obsessive compulsive disorder: serotonin and beyond</article-title><source>Am J Psychiatry</source><volume>155</volume><fpage>92</fpage><lpage>100</lpage></citation></ref></ref-list></back><floats-wrap><table-wrap id="t1-ndt-3-687" position="float"><label>Table 1</label><caption><p>Common obsessions and compulsions</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Common obsessions</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Common compulsions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>OCD</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Contamination</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Washing</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Repeated doubts</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Cleaning</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Need for order or symmetry</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Counting</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Aggressive/violent obsessions</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Checking</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sexual imagery</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Ordering</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Demanding reassurances</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Repetitive actions</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>TS</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Mental play</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Touching</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Perfectionism</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Self-injurious behavior</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Aggressive/violent obsessions</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Need for order or symmetry</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn1-ndt-3-687"><p><bold>References:</bold> (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b6-ndt-3-687">Cath et al 2001</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b14-ndt-3-687">George et al 1993</xref>; <xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b19-ndt-3-687">Holzer et al 1994</xref>).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t2-ndt-3-687" position="float"><label>Table 2</label><caption><p>Complex motor tics, compulsive tics, impulsive tics, impulsive-compulsive tics and schizo-obsessive symptoms</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="groups"><thead><tr><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Complex motor tics</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Compulsive tics</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Impulsive tics</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Impulsive-compulsive tics</th><th align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Schizo-obsessive symptoms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Definition</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Coordinated, purposeless involuntary movements that can resemble voluntary activity</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Repetitive movements performed according to rules (ie, ritualistic), in response to an obsession or to reduce tension</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Repetitive movements performed without forethought and without regard to consequences. Often socially unacceptable</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Repetitive movements with qualities of both compulsive and impulsive tics</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Repetitive thoughts or beliefs that appear psychotic in nature</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Examples</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Skipping, hopping, rubbing, jumping, smelling</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Touching a door a certain number of times; smelling fingers to check for contamination</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Touching self or others; touching a hot stove; stepping into traffic</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Hitting someone a certain number of times; Pushing someone after they cough to avoid contamination</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Thoughts that a &#x0201c;voice&#x0201d; in the patient&#x02019;s &#x0201c;head&#x0201d; is telling him/her what to do; imaginary friends</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Treatments</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Alpha-adrenergic agonists, antipsychotics</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">SSRI&#x02019;s, antipsychotics, cognitive-behavioral therapy</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Antipsychotics, alpha-adrenergic agonists, mood stabilizers, stimulants</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Antipsychotics, alpha-adrenergic agonists, SSRI&#x02019;s, mood stabilizers, stimulants, cognitive-behavioral therapy</td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Antipsychotics, SSRI&#x02019;s, cognitive-behavioral therapy (CBT)</td></tr></tbody></table></table-wrap><table-wrap id="t3-ndt-3-687" position="float"><label>Table 3</label><caption><p>Common impulsive compulsions</p></caption><table frame="hsides" rules="none"><tbody><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Males</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pathological gambling</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Sexual addictions</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Pyromania</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Intermittent explosive disorder</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"><bold>Females</bold></td><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Kleptomania</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Trichotillomania</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Binge eating</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Shopping/buying</td></tr><tr><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1"/><td align="left" rowspan="1" colspan="1">Self-injurious behavior</td></tr></tbody></table><table-wrap-foot><fn id="tfn2-ndt-3-687"><p><bold>Reference:</bold> (<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="b17-ndt-3-687">Hollander et al 2000</xref>).</p></fn></table-wrap-foot></table-wrap></floats-wrap></article>